8. test_k_odd_means_split_move_gives_Gx | requirement: When k is odd, replacing an even pile 2x with k piles of x yields Grundy g(x) because XOR of an odd number of identical values is g(x). | technique: Derived property from rules: for odd k, the split move from an even pile leads to Grundy g(x). Verified by DP. | oracle: The reachable set for an even pile must contain g(x) when k is odd. | validity: Follows from XOR properties. Guarded on odd k and even pile sizes ≤20. | value: Detects errors in odd‑k split handling.
9. test_remove_one_move_always_present | requirement: From any non‑empty pile, removing one cow is always a legal move. Therefore the Grundy value of a pile must be different from the Grundy value of the pile‑size‑minus‑1 (because mex ensures it differs unless other moves also produce that value). | technique: Invariant: g(a) ≠ g(a‑1) unless there exists another move reaching g(a‑1). We check that for each a≤20, g(a) is not equal to g(a‑1) unless g(a‑1) is also reachable by a split move. | oracle: If g(a) == g(a‑1) and g(a‑1) is not in the split‑move reachable set, the DP is inconsistent. | validity: The mex operation ensures g(a) is the smallest non‑negative integer not in the reachable set. If g(a‑1) is not reachable, then g(a) must be ≤ g(a‑1). But since g(a‑1) is not reachable, g(a) could equal g(a‑1) only if g(a‑1) is also not in the set—which is impossible because remove‑one adds g(a‑1). So the property holds for any correct Grundy computation. | value: Catches DP implementation errors in the test itself, increasing confidence in oracles that use DP.
